-- postpartum week 69: Eating well when your toddler eats a vegetarian diet
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   69,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler eats a vegetarian diet',
   '"A vegetarian plate can be a complete plate — it just asks for a little more intention."',
   'This week primarily covers a vegetarian diet that may include dairy and may or may not include eggs. A fully vegan diet can also support growth when carefully planned, but it requires a different approach to vitamin B12 and closer attention to several other nutrients. A few nutrients deserve deliberate attention. Plant iron is absorbed less efficiently than iron from meat, so regular iron-rich foods and vitamin-C pairings matter. Vitamin B12 occurs naturally in animal-derived foods and is also available through fortified foods and supplements; whether dairy and eggs provide enough depends on the foods and quantities actually eaten. Vitamin D, iodine, zinc, calcium and omega-3 fats may also need attention depending on the family''s food choices, fortification practices and local guidance. Plant foods such as ground flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts and certain oils provide ALA omega-3. The body converts only a limited amount to DHA, but that does not mean every vegetarian toddler automatically requires a DHA supplement. An algae-derived DHA product can be discussed with the child''s clinician when the overall diet warrants it. Many vegetarian toddlers can obtain adequate protein from varied meals containing foods such as dal, beans, soy foods, dairy, eggs, nut or seed pastes and grains. Protein sources do not need to be paired within the same meal, but adequate total food and energy intake still matter — especially for a child who eats small quantities or has a restricted range. A well-planned vegetarian diet can support normal growth. Growth depends on the diet''s energy density, variety, nutrient coverage and the child''s overall intake — not simply whether meat is absent. Follow your child''s individual growth pattern rather than expecting them to be either leaner or heavier because they are vegetarian.',
   'If your child seems unusually pale, tired, or has a noticeably reduced appetite: mention this to your paediatrician — it''s worth checking iron levels rather than assuming it will resolve on its own. If your child eats little or no dairy or egg: and does not regularly consume a reliably fortified food, discuss B12 intake and supplementation with your paediatrician or a registered dietitian. Dairy and eggs can contribute B12, while appropriately fortified cereals, dairy alternatives or nutritional yeast may provide additional reliable sources — check labels, because fortification varies. If your child drinks a lot of cow''s milk: for many children aged 12–24 months, about 16 ounces — or two cups — of plain whole cow''s milk a day is a useful guide unless their clinician recommends differently. Regularly exceeding 24 ounces can crowd out iron-rich foods. Curd, paneer and cheese also contribute to overall dairy intake, so milk is not the only dairy source. If your family follows a fully vegan diet: your child needs a reliable vitamin B12 source through an appropriately dosed supplement or consistently fortified food — this shouldn''t be left to chance. Vitamin D, iodine, calcium, iron, zinc, protein, energy intake and omega-3 sources also need review. Ask a paediatrician or registered dietitian familiar with paediatric vegan diets to assess the complete pattern rather than adding several supplements independently. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. Egg and dairy are useful foods when included, but neither must appear at every meal. Across the week, rotate dal, beans, chickpeas, soy foods where culturally acceptable, dairy, egg, nut or seed pastes and varied grains. The goal is dietary coverage across time, not proving that every plate is complete on its own. If you include egg Egg is optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely if egg isn''t part of your family''s diet. When you do include it, cook it fully: no runny yolks.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "There''s nothing incomplete about raising your child vegetarian. A well-planned vegetarian diet can fully meet a toddler''s needs — the operative word is planned. This week is about the handful of things worth being deliberate about, and the reassurance that most of it, you''re probably already doing. Every choice below is vegetarian. Option A leaves out egg; option B includes it — pick whichever fits your family, or mix and match freely across the week.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi with finely ground peanuts or smooth peanut paste", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable and egg bhurji with toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer curry with rice and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted almonds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted almonds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti with a moong dal and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What''s genuinely at risk — and what''s not", "notes": "A few nutrients deserve deliberate attention on a vegetarian diet: iron (plant iron absorbs less easily than the iron in meat), vitamin B12 (occurs naturally in animal foods and is also available through fortified foods and supplements — whether dairy and eggs provide enough depends on the foods and quantities actually eaten), zinc (grains and legumes contain compounds that block some of its absorption), and omega-3 fats, where plant sources provide ALA but the body converts only a limited amount to DHA. For your child: Many vegetarian toddlers can obtain adequate protein from varied meals containing foods such as dal, beans, soy foods, dairy, eggs, nut or seed pastes and grains. Protein sources don''t need to be paired within the same meal, but adequate total food and energy intake still matter — especially for a child who eats small quantities or has a restricted range. Prepare all shared foods for your child''s developmental stage: cook beans until very soft and mash or flatten them as needed, finely grind nuts and seeds or use smooth pastes, remove all hard pieces and pits, and keep your child seated upright and continuously supervised while eating.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla with grated vegetables", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal chilla with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of nutmeg", "dinner_a": "Roti with a masoor dal and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry with roti", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Making iron work harder", "notes": "Plant iron absorbs better alongside vitamin C — a squeeze of lemon over dal, a tomato-based curry, or a piece of citrus fruit soon after a meal all help. Do not serve caffeinated tea or coffee to a toddler at all. For adults, having tea or coffee with an iron-rich meal can reduce absorption, so having them between meals may be preferable when iron status is a concern. For your child: Dal, rajma, chana, and leafy greens are all useful plant iron sources — pairing them with something vitamin-C-rich in the same meal is a small habit that adds up.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla with grated carrot", "breakfast_b": "Besan chilla with a masala omelette on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-cauliflower curry with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted cashews for the adult; do not offer whole roasted cashews to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana-methi dal and a tomato sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti and a tomato sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Where dairy and eggs help — and where they don''t reach", "notes": "Milk, curd, paneer, and cheese can contribute calcium, B12, and protein — dairy and eggs can contribute vitamin B12, while appropriately fortified cereals, dairy alternatives or nutritional yeast may provide additional reliable sources; check labels, because fortification varies. Egg adds protein, B12, and a little DHA, but whether these foods add up to enough depends on the amounts your child actually eats, not just whether they''re on the plate somewhere in the week. Milk is genuinely useful here, but it can crowd out iron-rich foods if it''s offered too freely — the usual guidance is around two cups a day for a toddler this age, with meals built around food first. For you: If your child rarely eats egg and dairy intake is inconsistent, mention it to your paediatrician — a fortified food or a targeted supplement may be worth discussing rather than assuming it will average out.", "breakfast_a": "Unsweetened ragi porridge with milk and mashed fruit", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted peanuts for the adult; use smooth peanut butter spread thinly if sharing", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti with a rajma-carrot curry", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry with roti", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Getting more from grains and legumes", "notes": "Soaking, germinating, fermenting or leavening some grains and legumes may reduce phytate and improve mineral availability, but the effect varies by food and preparation. These techniques are optional kitchen tools — not requirements for making a vegetarian diet adequate. Cook legumes thoroughly for digestibility and safety, and cook all sprouts until steaming hot before serving them to a young child. This is a small kitchen habit, not a strict rule — even without soaking, a varied vegetarian diet still delivers real nutrition. Soaking just tilts things a little further in your child''s favour.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia upma", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia upma with a fully cooked fried egg, firm white and yolk", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-pumpkin curry with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of grapes, quartered lengthwise, with roasted pistachios for the adult; keep whole pistachios entirely separate from the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with fully hydrated chia seeds mixed through it", "dinner_a": "Roti with a moong-beetroot dal", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to loop in your paediatrician", "notes": "A well-planned vegetarian diet can support normal growth. Growth depends on the diet''s energy density, variety, nutrient coverage and your child''s overall intake — not simply whether meat is absent. Follow your child''s individual growth pattern rather than expecting them to be either leaner or heavier because they are vegetarian. Worth knowing: If your family follows a fully vegan diet, your child needs a reliable vitamin B12 source through an appropriately dosed supplement or consistently fortified food — this shouldn''t be left to chance. Vitamin D, iodine, calcium, iron, zinc, protein, energy intake and omega-3 sources also need review. Ask a paediatrician or registered dietitian familiar with paediatric vegan diets to assess the complete pattern rather than adding several supplements independently. For your child: Mention it to your paediatrician if your child seems consistently underweight, unusually tired or pale, or if dairy and egg intake is inconsistent enough that you''re unsure whether B12 needs are being met. These are the situations where a targeted supplement — iron, B12, vitamin D, or an algae-based DHA — might be worth discussing, rather than a general multivitamin by default.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat vegetable uttapam with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat vegetable uttapam with a fully cooked fried egg on the side, firm white and yolk", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma-tomato curry", "lunch_b": "Egg-and-tomato curry with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of mashed custard apple, deseeded, with roasted sunflower seeds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted sunflower seeds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of plain milk", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana dal and a brinjal sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-brinjal curry with roti", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, did egg, dairy, or a vitamin-C pairing show up as often as you thought they would — or is there one small habit worth carrying forward?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to trust that a thoughtful vegetarian plate is already doing most of the work — and to spend your attention on the few habits that make the real difference, rather than everything at once?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 70: Eating well when your toddler's appetite drops
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   70,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler''s appetite drops',
   '"A smaller appetite can be normal when growth slows — but your child''s growth pattern still matters."',
   'Somewhere around their first birthday, most toddlers'' appetites drop noticeably — and it''s one of the most common feeding worries parents raise with a paediatrician. It reflects genuine biology, not a problem with your child or your cooking. Energy needs vary by body size, activity and growth, and for a healthy child who is tracking appropriately, you generally don''t need to calculate calories — the drop itself simply reflects growth that has slowed to a fraction of the infant rate. A useful way to share the work: you decide what food is offered, and when and where it''s offered. Your child decides whether to eat, and how much. Trying to take over their half — through pressure, bargaining, or bribery — can increase mealtime tension and may interfere with your child''s ability to respond to their own hunger and fullness cues. Toddler intake can vary substantially from one meal or day to the next, and a diet that looks thin on any single day usually balances out over the week. A loose rhythm — regular meal and snack times, a flexible but predictable mealtime, water in between — tends to help more than any individual food choice. A genuine drop in appetite is different from a genuine drop in growth, and this week ends with the specific signs — from weight loss to signs of dehydration — that are worth a paediatrician''s attention rather than more patience, judged against your own child''s growth trajectory rather than a fixed timeline.',
   'If your child has lost weight, rather than just gained more slowly: mention this to your paediatrician. A slower gain is common and expected at this age; an actual loss is worth checking rather than waiting out. If milk or juice makes up a large part of your child''s day: around 16 ounces — or two cups — of plain whole milk a day is a useful guide for many children aged 12-24 months; avoid regularly exceeding 24 ounces, because milk can crowd out iron-rich foods. Continued breastfeeding and dairy foods such as curd and paneer are also part of the overall pattern. Juice, if offered, should be 100% juice, kept to a maximum of about 4 ounces and ideally served with a meal — whole fruit remains preferable. Both milk and juice can genuinely blunt appetite for food when offered more freely than this. If mealtimes have become a source of daily stress or bargaining: it''s worth deliberately stepping back from pressure, rewards, or bribery around food for a few weeks and holding a calm, predictable routine instead — the evidence suggests this helps more than it might feel like it will. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this means abandoning good food — it means offering it without a scoreboard. A calm plate your child sometimes leaves half full is doing its job.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook meat and fish all the way through, and cook eggs until both the white and yolk are fully set.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Somewhere around a year old, most toddlers'' appetites visibly shrink — and it can feel alarming after months of watching them eat eagerly. It isn''t usually a problem to fix. In their first year, babies roughly triple their birth weight; from age one to three, that growth rate slows dramatically, to only a few pounds and a few inches a year. A body growing that much more slowly genuinely needs less food. This week is about recalibrating your expectations to match your child''s real needs, and knowing the few situations where a slower appetite is worth a closer look. Trust your child''s appetite to do its job. Children this age are generally very good at eating as much as their bodies actually need, across a day or a week — even when a single meal looks like almost nothing. Prepare shared foods for your child''s stage: keep chicken very tender, finely shredded, and free of bone and cartilage; check fish thoroughly for bones and flake it before serving; and cook beans and legumes until very soft, mashing or flattening them as needed.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with finely ground peanuts or smooth peanut paste", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana dal and a bhindi sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a bhindi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What''s normal — and what''s not", "notes": "Toddler intake can vary substantially from one meal or day to the next without automatically indicating a growth problem. Your child might eat everything in sight at breakfast and barely touch lunch or dinner, or want the same food for three days running and then refuse it outright the fourth. None of that is a red flag on its own. What matters is the pattern across several days and weeks, not any single plate. For your child: Growth spurts, teething, a minor cold, more physical activity, or simply a more interesting distraction in the room can all shift how much your child eats on a given day — and it usually evens out on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli with grated carrot", "breakfast_b": "Oats idli with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a beans-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a beans-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A slice of watermelon with roasted cashews for the adult; do not offer whole roasted cashews to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of saffron", "dinner_a": "Roti with a toor dal and a capsicum sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a capsicum sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Who decides what, and who decides how much", "notes": "A useful way to think about mealtimes: you decide what food is offered, when, and where. Your child decides whether to eat, and how much. Trying to control their side — coaxing, bargaining, one-more-bite pressure, distraction to sneak in extra food — can increase mealtime tension and may interfere with your child''s ability to respond to their own hunger and fullness cues. For your child: It''s normal to feel uneasy watching a plate go back half full. Holding the line on your own role — offering a balanced plate, without pressure either way — is usually more effective than it feels in the moment.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a masala omelette on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a ridge gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and a ridge gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A kiwi with roasted almonds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted almonds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted watermelon seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a rajma curry and a lauki-tomato sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti and a lauki-tomato sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What quietly blunts appetite", "notes": "A few everyday habits can dull appetite without you noticing: milk or juice sipped freely through the day, snacking that never really stops between meals, or dessert and screens offered as a reward for eating. Around 16 ounces — or two cups — of plain whole milk a day is a useful guide for many children aged 12-24 months; avoid regularly exceeding 24 ounces, because milk can crowd out iron-rich foods. Continued breastfeeding and dairy foods such as curd and paneer are also part of the overall pattern. Juice, if offered at all, should be 100% juice, kept to a maximum of about 4 ounces, ideally with a meal — whole fruit remains the better choice. Water is the right choice between scheduled meals and snacks. For you: If snacking happens constantly through the day, try shifting to a small number of set snack times instead — it gives appetite room to build before the next meal.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi with finely ground roasted peanuts", "breakfast_b": "Sabudana khichdi with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of papaya with roasted pistachios for the adult; keep whole pistachios entirely separate from the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk with a pinch of turmeric", "dinner_a": "Roti with a moong dal and a carrot-peas sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a carrot-peas sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Structure that helps, without pressure", "notes": "A loose rhythm helps more than any single food choice: meals and snacks roughly every two-and-a-half to three hours, and a mealtime that lasts somewhere around 20 to 30 minutes, are useful starting examples rather than fixed rules — adjust them to your child''s pace and developmental needs. When a meal has clearly run its course, let your child know a few minutes ahead, then clear the plate calmly and without comment; the next meal or snack isn''t far off. Small starting portions are genuinely fine — a spoonful or two of each food is a realistic serving, not a sign your child isn''t getting enough. Include at least one food your child usually accepts at every meal, alongside whatever else is on offer. It takes the pressure off any single plate and keeps mealtimes calmer for everyone.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina uttapam with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Semolina uttapam with a fully cooked fried egg on the side, firm white and yolk", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma-tomato curry and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of grapes, quartered lengthwise, with roasted sunflower seeds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted sunflower seeds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of soft-cooked, stewed and mashed apricot", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana-methi dal and a beetroot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to loop in your paediatrician", "notes": "Many toddlers with variable appetites continue to track appropriately on their growth curve — the mismatch is often between a parent''s memory of an eager infant appetite and a toddler''s genuinely smaller need, not a sign of undereating. Contact your paediatrician for: weight loss, a downward shift from your child''s established growth pattern, persistent appetite reduction, frequent gagging or choking, pain with eating, repeated vomiting, chronic diarrhoea or constipation, unusual fatigue, or an extremely restricted range of foods. Seek urgent medical advice if your child cannot keep liquids down, is urinating markedly less than usual, has a dry mouth or no tears when crying, or seems unusually sleepy or hard to rouse — these can be signs of dehydration. For your child: Your paediatrician tracks your child''s growth over time using their own curve, not a single weigh-in — that''s the most reliable way to tell a genuine concern from an ordinary toddler slowdown.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a fully cooked fried egg on the side, firm white and yolk", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a drumstick sabzi, soft pulp only, with the pod fibres fully removed", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and a drumstick sabzi, soft pulp only, with the pod fibres fully removed", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of mashed custard apple, deseeded, with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of cinnamon", "dinner_a": "Roti with a toor dal and a bhindi-tomato sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry with roti", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Looking back at the week, was there a meal where you found yourself coaxing or bargaining — and what might it look like to offer the same plate calmly instead, and let your child decide the rest?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to measure your child''s eating over a week instead of a single plate — and to trust that a smaller appetite, on its own, is not a smaller amount of care from you?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 71: Eating well when your toddler has a food allergy
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   71,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler has a food allergy',
   '"Avoiding one food doesn''t mean losing a whole way of eating together."',
   'Food allergy affects many children worldwide, although the foods most often involved vary by country, age and local eating patterns. The foundation of safe eating is following your child''s exact avoidance and exposure plan from their allergist — not a general list — alongside checking ingredient labels every time you buy a product, since formulations can change without notice. Cross-contact is the everyday risk to manage at home and when eating out: shared utensils, cutting boards, or kitchen equipment can carry enough residue to trigger a reaction even when the allergen itself isn''t on the plate. Avoiding a major food, especially milk, can leave a real nutritional gap if it isn''t deliberately replaced — this week covers practical substitutions and when a registered dietitian is worth bringing in. Every family managing a food allergy should have a written emergency plan from their allergist, know that a severe reaction can occur without any hives, and understand that epinephrine should be used without hesitation when in doubt. This week also covers how allergies can change over time and why any reassessment belongs with your allergist, never trialled at home. Substitution key This week''s chart is a general base menu, not filtered to any single child''s allergies. Each meal option below is tagged with its major allergens so you can see at a glance what to swap — every substitute still needs to be checked against your child''s other allergies before it''s served. - Milk, curd or paneer → a clinician-approved fortified alternative chosen with your child''s doctor or dietitian, plus another tolerated protein and fat source. - Egg → a tolerated dal, bean, tofu (only if soy is confirmed safe), meat, or fish option, depending on what else applies to your child. - Peanut, tree nut, or seed → omit it, or use a separately confirmed tolerated alternative — never assume another nut or seed is automatically safe. - Wheat-based roti, dhokla, paratha or missi roti → an appropriate tolerated grain preparation, checked for cross-contact. - Fish → a tolerated vegetarian, egg, or meat protein option.',
   'If your child has a confirmed milk allergy: choose an unsweetened, appropriately fortified alternative with your paediatrician or dietitian for calcium, vitamin D and protein. Some children with milk allergy also react to soy, so don''t introduce a soy-based substitute automatically — confirm it''s appropriate first. If your child has more than one diagnosed food allergy: ask your paediatrician about a referral to a registered dietitian — managing several restrictions at once raises the chance of a nutritional gap appearing without either of you noticing. If your child has an allergist-approved plan for a specific tolerated form: such as baked milk or baked egg, or a supervised desensitisation plan, follow it exactly as written — general guidance like this document doesn''t override it. If a label''s wording is unclear or you''re unsure about cross-contact: treat it as a real caution, check against your allergist''s advice on precautionary wording, and choose a different product if safety can''t be confirmed. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this is about fear — it''s about precision. Knowing exactly what to avoid, and trusting that everything else is genuinely fine, is what makes ordinary mealtimes possible again. Avoiding one confirmed allergen doesn''t make your child''s whole diet deficient, or set their plate apart from everyone else''s at the table.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely, and use the substitution key above for anything else that applies to your child''s confirmed allergen. When you do include meat, fish or egg, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A diagnosed food allergy changes how you shop and cook, but it doesn''t have to shrink your child''s world at the table. Follow the exact avoidance and exposure plan your allergist has given you — some children have allergist-approved plans for a tolerated form, such as baked milk or baked egg, so don''t broaden or relax what''s avoided based on general guidance like this one. Unnecessary restriction beyond your child''s actual plan can narrow nutrition and daily life, but any decision about related or cross-reactive foods belongs in that individual plan. This week''s meals are a general base menu, not allergen-free for any specific allergen. Each option is tagged with its major allergens — use the substitution key above and Day 4''s guidance to adapt it to your child, and lean on your allergist''s plan over any general list, including this one. A general reminder for shared foods at this age: keep chicken very tender and finely shredded, free of bone and cartilage; check fish thoroughly for bones and flake it; and cook beans and legumes until very soft, mashing or flattening them as needed.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla with coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dhokla with a boiled egg on the side (contains egg)", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a tinda sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a tinda sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of chikoo with roasted almonds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted almonds to the young child (contains tree nut)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted flaxseed (contains milk)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a moong dal and a methi sabzi (contains wheat)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a methi sabzi (contains wheat, fish)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (contains milk)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Reading labels every time", "notes": "Check the ingredient label on every product, every time you buy it — manufacturers reformulate without warning, so a brand that was safe last month isn''t guaranteed to be safe today. Allergen-labelling rules and precautionary wording vary by country: learn the required allergen names and advisory phrasing where you live — in India, FSSAI requires specified allergens to be declared as \"Contains...\" and permits cross-contact wording such as \"May contain...\" — check the entire label every time, and follow your allergist''s advice about how to treat precautionary statements. For your child: If a label''s wording is unclear, contacting the manufacturer can help, but it isn''t a guarantee of certainty — if safety can''t be confirmed, choose a different product.", "breakfast_a": "Poha with finely ground peanuts or smooth peanut paste, only if peanut is a confirmed tolerated food for your child (contains peanut)", "breakfast_b": "Poha with a masala omelette on the side (contains egg)", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a parwal sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a parwal sabzi (contains egg)", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of pomegranate for the adult. For the young child, offer only if appropriate for their current chewing skills and prepare it in a developmentally suitable form, supervising closely while eating. Serve roasted walnuts only to the adult; keep whole nuts away from the child (contains tree nut)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of cardamom (contains milk)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a masoor dal and a potato-peas sabzi (contains wheat)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a potato-peas sabzi (contains wheat)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg (contains egg)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Keeping the kitchen safe", "notes": "At home, cross-contact is the everyday risk to manage: a knife, cutting board, or pan used for the allergen and then wiped rather than washed can carry enough residue to trigger a reaction. Wash hands with soap and water before preparing or serving your child''s food, clean preparation surfaces thoroughly, use separate utensils and storage where your child''s plan calls for it, and wash shared equipment properly between uses rather than assuming a quick rinse is enough. When eating out, tell kitchen staff about the allergy directly and specifically each visit, and avoid shared fryers, buffets, or bakery displays where cross-contact can''t reasonably be assessed — a restaurant that was safe once isn''t automatically safe the next time, since staff, suppliers, and prep routines change. For your child: It''s reasonable to ask a restaurant how a dish is prepared, whether shared equipment is used, and to skip anything the kitchen can''t clearly confirm.", "breakfast_a": "Missi roti with curd (contains wheat, milk)", "breakfast_b": "Missi roti with a fully cooked fried egg on the side, firm white and yolk (contains wheat, egg)", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a karela sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and a karela sabzi (contains fish)", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of muskmelon with roasted cashews for the adult; do not offer whole roasted cashews to the young child (contains tree nut)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted sunflower seeds (contains milk)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana-methi dal and a sweet potato sabzi (contains wheat)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti and a sweet potato sabzi (contains wheat, egg)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (contains milk)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Making sure nutrition still adds up", "notes": "Avoiding a major food can leave a gap worth filling deliberately. Milk allergy is usually the biggest one to plan around: toddlers need reliable sources of calcium, vitamin D, protein, energy and dietary fat. Cow''s milk can provide several of these nutrients, so when it''s excluded, the overall diet and any replacement drink should be chosen deliberately. Plant-based drinks vary substantially in energy, protein, fat and fortification, so none should be assumed an automatic equivalent — choose an unsweetened, appropriately fortified option with your paediatrician or dietitian, and check fortification levels on the label since they vary by brand. Worth knowing: Some children with cow''s-milk allergy also react to soy, particularly in certain non-IgE gastrointestinal conditions. Do not introduce soy as the automatic replacement; confirm an appropriate alternative with your child''s allergist or paediatrician. Egg and wheat avoidance are generally easier to work around nutritionally, using other protein sources and fortified allergen-free grains.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with sambar and a boiled egg on the side (contains egg)", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a cabbage-peas sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a cabbage-peas sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of mango with roasted pistachios for the adult; keep whole pistachios entirely separate from the young child (contains tree nut)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk with a pinch of black pepper (contains milk)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a moong dal and a beans sabzi (contains wheat)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a beans sabzi (contains wheat, fish)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (contains milk)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Being ready for a reaction", "notes": "Every child with a diagnosed food allergy should have a written emergency action plan from their allergist, shared with every caregiver — you, family, and daycare — and followed exactly as written. A severe reaction can happen without any hives or skin symptoms at all, so judge severity by the full picture: trouble breathing or a hoarse voice, swelling of the tongue or lips, repeated vomiting, or your child turning pale, faint, or unusually limp, as well as skin symptoms when they do appear. If you suspect anaphylaxis or are unsure whether the prescribed epinephrine auto-injector is needed, administer it promptly and call local emergency services. Antihistamines do not replace epinephrine. For your child: Keep the prescribed device within its expiry date, stored at the recommended temperature, and accessible wherever your child goes — and make sure every caregiver, including family and daycare, knows how to use your child''s specific device. After using an epinephrine auto-injector, call your local emergency services immediately and go to a hospital for monitoring, even if your child seems to recover quickly — follow your written plan regarding a possible second dose, since a delayed second wave of symptoms can follow the first.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with tomato chutney (contains wheat)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a boiled egg on the side (contains wheat, egg)", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a cauliflower-peas sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a cauliflower-peas sabzi (contains egg)", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of papaya with roasted sesame seeds for the adult; finely grind sesame seeds for the young child (contains sesame)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted watermelon seeds (contains milk)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a rajma curry and a spinach sabzi (contains wheat)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a spinach sabzi (contains wheat)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg (contains egg)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Looking ahead with your allergist", "notes": "How likely a childhood food allergy is to fade with time varies substantially from child to child. Milk, egg, wheat and soy allergies are more often outgrown than peanut, tree-nut, fish or shellfish allergies — but milk and egg allergy specifically can persist well beyond age five, sometimes into adolescence or adulthood, so there''s no fixed timeline to expect. Whether or not your child''s allergy is one that''s more likely to resolve, reassessment is always your allergist''s call, made through testing and, when appropriate, a supervised food challenge in a clinical setting equipped to treat a reaction. Never test a confirmed allergen at home, even if your child seems to have grown out of it. For your child: If your allergist ever clears a food after a supervised challenge, ask about how often it should be eaten afterward — regular exposure is sometimes advised to help keep tolerance in place.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk and mashed banana (contains milk)", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked fried egg on the side, firm white and yolk (contains egg)", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a tomato-bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and a tomato-bhindi sabzi (contains fish)", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of watermelon with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child (contains tree nut)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of dry ginger powder (contains milk)", "dinner_a": "Roti with a toor dal and a carrot-methi sabzi (contains wheat)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot-methi curry with roti (contains wheat, milk)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (contains milk)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Is your child''s written emergency plan up to date and in the hands of everyone who feeds or cares for them — and is there one label habit from this week worth keeping?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to hold this precisely — confident about the one food to avoid, and just as confident that everything else on the table is genuinely safe?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 72: Eating well when your toddler is constipated
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   72,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler is constipated',
   '"Fibre, fluid and a calm routine can help — but persistent pain or withholding deserves proper support."',
   'Constipation is one of the most common reasons parents bring a toddler to the paediatrician, and the overwhelming majority of cases have no underlying medical cause. The shift from milk-based feeding to table food is a well-recognised trigger window at this age, because fibre and fluid intake can quietly drop just as stool needs to become bulkier and easier to pass. A useful rough fibre guide is your child''s age in years plus about five grams a day — around seven grams for a two-year-old — from everyday foods like fruit with the skin on, vegetables, beans, and whole grains, offered regularly rather than tracked precisely. Milk can remain part of a balanced diet, but large amounts may reduce appetite for fibre-rich food and other drinks; if constipation persists, it''s worth discussing total dairy intake with your paediatrician rather than cutting milk independently. A single painful bowel movement can start a stool-withholding cycle that makes the next one harder still. If your child is developmentally ready and comfortable sitting, a predictable, low-pressure potty or toilet routine with securely supported feet may help. Never force sitting when the child is frightened or resisting. These ordinary changes can help and are useful foundations, particularly when constipation is mild and recent. But persistent pain, withholding or hard stools may need a paediatrician-led treatment plan rather than a longer trial of food changes alone. This week also covers the specific signs — blood in the stool, severe pain, vomiting, fever, or no improvement despite genuine effort — that mean it''s time to bring your paediatrician in rather than keep adjusting alone.',
   'If your child drinks a lot of milk: the appropriate amount depends on your child''s age, breastfeeding, growth and the rest of their diet — for many young toddlers, roughly 16 to 24 ounces may fall within commonly used guidance, but dairy foods like curd and paneer also count toward overall intake. If constipation persists despite sensible changes, discuss total dairy intake with your paediatrician rather than removing milk independently. If stool withholding has become a pattern: treat it matter-of-factly and avoid pressure or punishment. Food, normal fluid intake and a calm routine can support recovery, but persistent withholding often needs a paediatrician-led plan to keep stools consistently soft while the fear and pain settle. If diet and routine changes haven''t helped after a real trial: talk to your paediatrician about next steps, which may include a stool softener used under their guidance. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this is about getting it perfect. Small food and routine changes are useful foundations, but needing medical support doesn''t mean the family has failed.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Constipation is one of the most common reasons parents bring a toddler to the paediatrician, and in the vast majority of cases there''s no underlying medical problem — it''s a mix of diet, fluids, and sometimes a bit of behaviour that''s easing this age up. Mild, recent constipation may improve with small, steady changes. When pain or withholding has taken hold, medical treatment may be needed to keep stools soft long enough for the cycle to settle. This week focuses on supportive food, normal fluid intake and calm routines, alongside recognising when your paediatrician should take the lead. Prepare all fruit and vegetables in a texture appropriate for your child''s current chewing skills. Remove hard seeds or pits, cut slippery or firm pieces safely, and supervise while eating.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats khichdi", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats khichdi with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear, skin on, with roasted almonds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted almonds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana dal and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why this happens at this age", "notes": "The shift from milk-based feeding to table food is a common trigger window for constipation, because it''s easy for fibre and fluids to quietly drop just as stool needs to become bulkier. Add in a diet that''s naturally lower in fibre, not quite enough fluid through the day, a lot of milk, or simply less running-around than usual, and the combination adds up. None of this is about doing something wrong — it''s just where the pieces tend to line up at this stage. For your child: If a bowel movement was painful once, your child may start holding it in to avoid repeating that — which only makes the next one harder. Recognising this cycle early, rather than reading it as stubbornness, changes how you can help.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia with grated carrot", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia with a masala omelette on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a cabbage-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a cabbage-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of papaya with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of cardamom", "dinner_a": "Roti with a masoor dal and a methi sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a methi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fibre that actually helps", "notes": "One commonly used rough guide for children is age in years plus about five grams of fibre daily. It''s only a practical guide — not a target that needs to be counted precisely — and individual needs vary. Regularly offering fruit, vegetables, beans, lentils and whole grains is more useful than tracking every gram. Pears, prunes, and apples with the skin left on, berries, peas, sweet potato, beans and lentils, and whole grains are all good everyday sources. A clinician may sometimes suggest a small amount of prune, pear or apple juice for constipation, working through a natural sugar called sorbitol that draws water into the gut. Juice is optional rather than a daily requirement; for children aged one to three, keep total 100% fruit juice to no more than about four ounces a day unless your paediatrician advises differently. For your child: Adding fibre without also adding fluid can make things worse, not better, so pair the two together rather than increasing one alone.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with green peas", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a sweet potato sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and a sweet potato sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of stewed prunes with roasted cashews for the adult; do not offer whole roasted cashews to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted pistachios", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana-methi dal and a peas sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti and a peas sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Fluids, and where milk fits", "notes": "Fluid needs vary with age, body size, weather, activity, breastfeeding and illness. Offer water regularly with meals and between meals, and use your child''s thirst, urine colour and usual wet nappies or urination as practical guides. Food, milk and other appropriate drinks also contribute to total fluid intake, so there''s no need to force a fixed volume of plain water. Milk can remain part of a toddler''s balanced diet, but large amounts may reduce appetite for fibre-rich foods and other drinks. The appropriate amount depends on your child''s age, breastfeeding, growth and the rest of their diet — for many young toddlers, roughly 16 to 24 ounces of milk may fall within commonly used guidance, but dairy foods like curd and paneer also count toward overall intake. If constipation persists despite sensible changes, discuss your child''s total dairy intake with the paediatrician rather than removing milk independently. For you: Offering water alongside meals and through the day, rather than relying on milk to cover most of your child''s fluid needs, is a small habit that adds up. If you''re watching your child''s overall milk intake, the fruit-based nourishment break and the dal, egg, meat or fish meal options are easy ways to skip a dairy option rather than adding one at every turn.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable rava idli with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable rava idli with sambar and a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a broccoli sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a broccoli sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of orange segments with roasted pistachios for the adult; keep whole pistachios entirely separate from the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti with a moong dal and a beetroot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of leftover dal or vegetable oats porridge"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A calm toilet routine", "notes": "If your child is developmentally ready and already comfortable sitting on a potty or toilet, a short, relaxed sit after a meal can use the body''s natural after-meal reflex. Keep their feet flat and securely supported with an appropriate stool, and never force the sit. If toilet learning is creating fear or withholding, pause the pressure and discuss the pattern with your paediatrician. Keep the whole thing low-pressure — avoid negative language about accidents or withholding, since shame tends to make a child hold on tighter, not let go. For your child: Treat withholding matter-of-factly and avoid pressure or punishment. Food, normal fluid intake and a calm routine can support recovery, but persistent withholding often needs a paediatrician-led plan to keep stools consistently soft while the fear and pain settle.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk and mashed pear", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of guava with roasted sesame seeds for the adult; finely grind sesame seeds for the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted melon seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a rajma curry and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to loop in your paediatrician", "notes": "Give diet, fluid, and routine changes a real trial before expecting things to fully settle — but if they aren''t helping, your paediatrician has more options, including a stool softener when appropriate. Some paediatricians recommend an osmotic laxative such as polyethylene glycol for a period of weeks to break a stuck cycle; it''s generally considered safe and non-habit-forming when used as directed, but it''s a decision for your child''s doctor, not something to start on your own. For your child: Contact your paediatrician if constipation lasts more than about two weeks, repeatedly returns, or isn''t improving with home measures. Seek prompt medical assessment for blood in the stool or rectal bleeding, persistent vomiting, a swollen abdomen, constant or severe abdominal pain, poor weight gain or weight loss, fever with significant illness, or a child who appears unusually unwell.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat vegetable uttapam with tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat vegetable uttapam with a fully cooked fried egg on the side, firm white and yolk", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of chikoo with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of nutmeg", "dinner_a": "Roti with a toor dal and a carrot-peas sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot-peas curry with roti", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did fibre, fluid, or the toilet routine feel like the piece that mattered most this week — and is there one habit worth carrying forward?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to trust that small, steady changes to food and routine are already doing real work — without needing to watch every single day for proof?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 73: Eating well when your toddler is teething
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   73,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler is teething',
   '"A few uncomfortable days around a new tooth are normal — a fever, ongoing diarrhoea or dehydration usually mean something else is going on."',
   'Between about one and three years old, toddlers are cutting molars and canines — bigger, more uncomfortable teeth than the incisors that came in during infancy. A few days of soreness, drooling, gum-rubbing and a smaller appetite around a new tooth are common and usually short-lived. Teething does not reliably cause diarrhoea, vomiting or a rash, even though it''s often blamed for these — good evidence backs this. Teething may coincide with mild discomfort or a small temperature rise, but a measured temperature of 38°C/100.4°F or higher should not simply be attributed to teething. Treating unrelated illness as ''just teething'' risks missing something that actually needs attention. Gentle gum massage with a clean finger and a firm rubber teether chilled in the refrigerator are commonly recommended comfort measures. Chilled soft foods may also feel soothing — chilled yogurt, refrigerated fruit puree, or a chilled soft banana piece prepared for your child''s chewing skills. Lean softer for a few days if needed, then bring back your child''s usual textures once it passes. Avoid benzocaine or lidocaine teething products, homeopathic teething remedies, and amber, wood or marble necklaces — they offer little or no proven benefit and may cause serious harm. A short appetite dip doesn''t need chasing with whatever your child will eat; nutrient-dense, easy-texture foods and steady fluids carry them through until it passes.',
   'If your child refuses most solid food for a day or two: offer nutrient-dense, easy-texture options — yogurt, mashed dal, fully cooked egg, a thinned smoothie — and keep fluids going. A brief appetite dip can happen, but refusal that is prolonged, worsening or accompanied by illness symptoms deserves medical advice. If you''re not sure this is teething: watch for a measured fever of 38°C/100.4°F or higher, repeated vomiting, significant diarrhoea, a spreading rash, or symptoms lasting more than two to three days — these point to something other than a new tooth and are worth a call to your paediatrician rather than waiting it out. If your child reaches for teething gels, tablets or a necklace: skip benzocaine or lidocaine teething products, homeopathic teething remedies, and amber, wood or marble necklaces — they offer little or no proven benefit and may cause serious harm. Gentle gum massage with a clean finger or a firm rubber teether chilled, not frozen, in the refrigerator is a safer and effective alternative. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this is about getting it perfect. A few hard days around a new tooth are ordinary, and needing extra patience or medical reassurance doesn''t mean anything went wrong.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Molars and canines are cutting through in this stretch, and they push through more gum tissue than the front teeth that came in earlier — so a few days of soreness, drooling and a smaller appetite around a new tooth are common. It''s uncomfortable, not usually a big event, and appetite usually improves as the soreness settles. This week focuses on comfort measures that actually help, keeping nutrition and fluids steady through a rough stretch, and recognising when symptoms are probably not teething at all. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability — cut slippery fruit safely, remove hard seeds and membranes where needed, soften firm foods, check fish carefully for bones, and supervise all eating, especially when sore gums make your child distracted. Lean on cold and soft textures for comfort this week, chilled rather than frozen solid, but keep offering your child''s usual range of foods too, so chewing skills don''t stall.", "breakfast_a": "Soft suji porridge with mashed banana and no added sugar", "breakfast_b": "Soft suji porridge with mashed banana and a fully cooked egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and mashed potato", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and mashed potato", "nourishment_break_a": "Chilled mango puree with roasted almonds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted almonds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of chilled curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Khichdi with a spoon of ghee and mashed carrot", "dinner_b": "Egg bhurji with soft khichdi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of chilled curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why this happens at this age", "notes": "Between about one and three years old, the molars and canines come in — bigger teeth with more gum to push through than the incisors from infancy. It''s common for a tooth to cause a few uncomfortable days: extra drooling, gum-rubbing, a bit of fussiness, and less interest in effortful chewing. All twenty baby teeth are usually in by around age three. For your child: A cold, clean object to gnaw on — a chilled teething ring or a cold washcloth — often gives real relief, because cold has a genuine numbing effect on sore gums. Keep it chilled, not frozen solid.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semolina upma, soft-cooked", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semolina upma with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and mashed pumpkin", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and mashed pumpkin", "nourishment_break_a": "Chilled watermelon cubes with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled buttermilk with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft dal-rice khichdi with a spoon of ghee", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with soft rice", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg, mashed"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Cold and soft, for a few days", "notes": "Gentle gum massage with a clean finger and a firm rubber teether chilled — not frozen — in the refrigerator are commonly recommended comfort measures for sore gums. Chilled soft foods can feel soothing too: chilled yogurt, refrigerated fruit puree, or a chilled soft banana piece prepared for your child''s chewing skills. Keep anything cold rather than frozen solid, which can bruise sore gums or become a choking risk. Lean softer for a few days if needed, then bring back your child''s usual range of textures once the soreness passes, so chewing skills keep moving forward. For your child: A short flare doesn''t need a long-term change in how your child eats. A brief appetite dip can happen, but refusal that is prolonged, worsening or accompanied by illness symptoms deserves medical advice.", "breakfast_a": "Chilled fruit and yogurt bowl with mashed banana and papaya", "breakfast_b": "Chilled fruit and yogurt bowl with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and mashed sweet potato", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and mashed sweet potato", "nourishment_break_a": "Chilled mango or papaya puree with roasted cashews for the adult; do not offer whole roasted cashews to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of chilled curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Soft roti soaked in dal with a mashed lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with soft rice and a mashed lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of chilled curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Comfort measures worth avoiding", "notes": "Avoid benzocaine or lidocaine teething products, homeopathic teething remedies, and amber, wood or marble necklaces. These products offer little or no proven benefit and may cause serious harm. Use a clean finger for gentle gum massage or offer a firm rubber teether chilled — not frozen — in the refrigerator. For your child: A gum-sore, distracted eater is not the moment to relax on choking caution — cut round or firm foods small, avoid whole nuts and thick blobs of nut butter, and keep your child seated while eating.", "breakfast_a": "Soft-cooked vegetable dalia", "breakfast_b": "Soft-cooked vegetable dalia with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and mashed cauliflower", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and mashed cauliflower", "nourishment_break_a": "Chilled orange segments with roasted pistachios for the adult; keep whole pistachios entirely separate from the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled milk with a pinch of cardamom", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong-masoor dal and mashed beetroot", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with soft roti and mashed beetroot", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of chilled curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Keeping nutrition and fluids steady", "notes": "A short dip in appetite doesn''t need chasing with whatever your child will eat. Soft minced meat, mashed beans and lentils, and fully cooked egg can provide iron and protein in easy textures. Yogurt provides protein, calcium and energy when dairy is tolerated. Offer fluids regularly through the day: breast milk if you are still nursing, plain water, or an age-appropriate plain milk or fortified alternative. Use a specialised formula only when your child''s clinician has recommended it. A chilled smoothie or thinned yogurt can do double duty as comfort and hydration. For you: If mealtimes feel like a standoff for a few days, it can help to think of it as riding out a short stretch rather than a battle to win — appetite usually improves as the soreness settles.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk and mashed papaya", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a fully cooked scrambled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and mashed carrot", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and mashed carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of chilled mashed banana with roasted almonds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted almonds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of chilled curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted melon seeds", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a spoon of ghee and mashed spinach", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with soft khichdi and mashed spinach", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of mashed dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s probably not teething", "notes": "Teething does not reliably cause diarrhoea, vomiting or a rash — the evidence for this is well established, though it remains a common myth. Teething may coincide with mild discomfort or a small temperature rise, but a measured temperature of 38°C/100.4°F or higher should not simply be attributed to teething; it''s worth looking past teething rather than waiting it out, since a real fever points to a separate illness that may need attention. For your child: If your child has a measured fever of 38°C/100.4°F or higher, repeated vomiting, significant diarrhoea, a spreading rash or appears genuinely unwell, look beyond teething and contact your paediatrician. Contact your paediatrician for significantly fewer wet nappies or urination, a dry mouth, no tears when crying or unusual tiredness. Seek urgent medical care if your child is difficult to wake, cannot keep fluids down, has not urinated for an unusually long period, or appears severely dehydrated.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable rava idli with a thin sambar", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable rava idli with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana-moong dal and mashed bottle gourd", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and mashed bottle gourd", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of chilled papaya with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of chilled coconut water", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and mashed pumpkin", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with soft roti and mashed pumpkin", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of chilled curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did a particular comfort measure make the biggest difference this week — and is there anything about mealtimes that felt easier once you stopped chasing a full plate?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to trust that a rough few days, food refusal and all, is not a sign that something is wrong — just a passing part of growing new teeth?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
