-- postpartum week 74: Supporting your toddler's iron intake
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   74,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Supporting your toddler''s iron intake',
   '"Iron-rich foods support prevention and recovery — but confirmed low iron needs a paediatrician-led testing and treatment plan."',
   'This plan supports healthy iron intake and can accompany treatment, but it does not treat confirmed iron deficiency by itself. If a blood test shows low iron, follow the supplement dose and follow-up testing prescribed for your child. Toddlers are one of the groups most likely to run low on iron. The iron stores most full-term babies are born with generally support them for about the first six months; from then on, iron must increasingly come from complementary foods, fortified foods or a supplement when clinically recommended. The transition from iron-fortified formula to cow''s milk during the second year, alongside rapid growth and a bigger blood volume, is a common window for intake to quietly drop. Cow''s milk is low in iron, and large amounts can reduce a toddler''s appetite for iron-rich foods. Intake above about 24 ounces a day is a recognised risk factor for iron deficiency; the appropriate amount below that ceiling depends on age, breastfeeding, growth and the rest of the child''s diet. Heme iron from meat, poultry and fish is generally absorbed more readily than the non-heme iron in beans, lentils, greens and fortified cereal. Eggs can contribute iron and protein, but aren''t equivalent to meat for iron absorption. Pairing non-heme iron sources with a vitamin C food — citrus, tomato, bell pepper, strawberries — meaningfully improves absorption, and it''s an easy habit rather than something to measure precisely. Low iron often causes no obvious symptoms, which is why testing — not symptom-watching — is what actually confirms it. Screening commonly happens during late infancy or the second year; ask your paediatrician what''s appropriate for your child. Any iron supplement should come from your paediatrician, not be started on your own — iron is genuinely dangerous in overdose for young children, and worth storing carefully.',
   'If your child drinks a lot of milk: intake above about 24 ounces a day is a recognised risk factor for iron deficiency; the appropriate amount below that depends on your child''s age, breastfeeding, growth and the rest of their diet. Avoid letting milk replace meals or become the main response whenever your child is hungry. If your family eats a vegetarian diet: include plain, iron-fortified cereal (check the label for fortification level), lentils, beans, tofu and dark leafy greens regularly, and pair them with a vitamin C food at the same meal. Egg, if your family includes it, adds further iron and protein; dairy can stay part of the diet but shouldn''t displace iron-rich meals. If you''re considering an iron supplement for your child: use only what your paediatrician has specifically recommended and dosed — iron is genuinely dangerous in overdose for young children. Store all supplements and multivitamins well out of reach and out of sight. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. Ordinary food habits support your child''s iron intake, while testing and treatment are there when food alone is not enough. Needing a prescribed supplement does not mean you fed your child incorrectly.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "The iron stores most full-term babies are born with generally support them for about the first six months. From then onward, iron must increasingly come from complementary foods, fortified foods or a supplement when clinically recommended. The transition from iron-fortified formula to cow''s milk can create another vulnerable period during the second year, right as growth and blood volume are increasing quickly. This week focuses on iron-rich foods your child can actually absorb, where milk fits without crowding things out, and when it''s worth asking your paediatrician about testing. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability — mince or shred meat finely, mash beans and lentils, remove hard seeds, and supervise all eating. Cook eggs fully; prepare orange segments, kiwi, guava, melon and steamed vegetable sticks appropriately; and make hummus smooth rather than thick or sticky.", "breakfast_a": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared per the label, with sliced strawberries", "breakfast_b": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared per the label, with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a soft-cooked tomato-bell pepper sabzi", "lunch_b": "Minced chicken keema with rice and a soft-cooked tomato-bell pepper sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Orange segments with roasted almonds for the adult; do not offer whole roasted almonds to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana dal and a bell pepper sabzi", "dinner_b": "Minced mutton curry with roti and a bell pepper sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why this happens at this age", "notes": "Cow''s milk is low in iron, and large amounts can reduce a toddler''s appetite for iron-rich foods. Intake above about 24 ounces a day is a recognised risk factor for iron deficiency. The appropriate amount below that ceiling depends on age, breastfeeding, growth and the rest of the child''s diet. Avoid letting milk replace meals or become the main response whenever your child is hungry. For your child: A toddler who''s a big milk-drinker and a picky solids-eater is exactly the combination that tends to run low on iron — worth keeping an eye on, not worrying over.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia with mashed sweet potato", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry with rice and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of papaya with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a pinch of turmeric", "dinner_a": "Roti with a rajma curry and a broccoli sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti and a broccoli sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Iron your child can actually use", "notes": "Heme iron from meat, poultry and fish is generally absorbed more readily than the non-heme iron in beans, lentils, greens and fortified cereal. Eggs can contribute iron and protein, but shouldn''t be treated as equivalent to meat for iron absorption. Pairing a non-heme iron source with something high in vitamin C — citrus, tomato, bell pepper, strawberries — genuinely improves how much of that iron gets absorbed, and it''s a simple habit rather than a precise science: a squeeze of lemon on dal, or fruit alongside fortified cereal, is enough. For your child: Small amounts of finely minced meat blended into a familiar dish like a curry or meatballs can add meaningful iron without changing how a dish tastes very much.", "breakfast_a": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler porridge with no added sugar, prepared per the label, with chopped strawberries", "breakfast_b": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler porridge with no added sugar, prepared per the label, with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a lentil-tomato dal and a mashed potato", "lunch_b": "Minced beef curry with rice and a mashed potato", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of kiwi and orange segments with roasted cashews for the adult; do not offer whole roasted cashews to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "Hummus with very soft-steamed carrot pieces prepared for your child''s chewing skills", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana-spinach dal and a tomato sabzi", "dinner_b": "Shredded chicken curry with roti and a tomato sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of hummus"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Milk, and where it fits", "notes": "Milk can stay part of a balanced toddler diet below about 24 ounces a day, with the right amount for your child depending on age, breastfeeding, growth and the rest of their diet. Avoid letting milk replace meals or become the main response whenever your child is hungry, since that''s what mainly displaces iron-rich foods. For you: If milk has become the easiest way to fill a picky day, it can help to think of it as one part of the day''s nutrition rather than the anchor — a fruit or dal-based nourishment break can cover some of the same ground without displacing iron-rich meals.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with orange segments", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a boiled egg mixed in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and a bell pepper sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a bell pepper sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of watermelon with roasted pistachios for the adult; keep whole pistachios entirely separate from the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti with a rajma-tomato curry and a mashed sweet potato", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a mashed sweet potato", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Vegetarian meals and iron", "notes": "A well-planned vegetarian toddler diet can meet iron needs. Because plant iron is absorbed less readily, include iron-fortified cereal, beans, lentils, tofu or other tolerated iron-rich foods regularly, and pair them with vitamin C foods. If your family includes egg, it can contribute additional iron and protein. Dairy can remain part of the diet, but shouldn''t displace iron-rich meals. For your child: This isn''t about tracking iron precisely — it''s about making iron-rich plant foods a regular, ordinary part of meals rather than an occasional extra.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk and chopped strawberries", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a scrambled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a bell pepper-tomato sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice and a bell pepper-tomato sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of guava with roasted walnuts for the adult; do not offer whole roasted walnuts to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of finely ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti with a lentil-spinach dal and a tomato sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg bhurji with roti and a tomato sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of mashed dal"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to loop in your paediatrician", "notes": "Low iron in a toddler often causes no obvious symptoms at all. Screening practices vary, but iron-deficiency screening commonly occurs during late infancy or the second year. Timing may depend on whether your child primarily received breast milk or iron-fortified formula and on other risk factors. Ask your paediatrician what testing is appropriate now. For your child: If a supplement is ever recommended, use exactly the product, dose and schedule your paediatrician gives you, and keep it stored well out of your child''s reach — iron supplements and multivitamins are a genuine poisoning risk in young children, and look enough like candy to be tempting. Contact your paediatrician if you notice unusual tiredness, marked pallor, persistent poor appetite, a very restricted diet, or eating non-food substances such as dirt. These signs are not specific to iron deficiency, so testing — not symptoms alone — should guide diagnosis.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable rava idli with a tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable rava idli with a boiled egg, mashed, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor-tomato dal and a broccoli sabzi", "lunch_b": "Minced chicken curry with rice and a broccoli sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of mixed melon with roasted cashews for the adult; do not offer whole roasted cashews to the young child", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a few strands of saffron", "dinner_a": "Roti with a chana dal and a carrot-tomato sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with roti and a carrot-tomato sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did a particular iron-rich food or pairing feel easy to keep offering — and is there one small habit worth carrying into next week?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to trust that a handful of ordinary food habits are quietly doing real work for your child''s iron, without needing to track or measure any of it?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 75: Eating well when your toddler has a stomach bug
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   75,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler has a stomach bug',
   '"Small, frequent fluids and food as tolerated help your child recover faster than fasting or a restrictive diet ever would."',
   'Most toddler stomach bugs are viral, self-limited, and pass within about a week. With uncomplicated gastroenteritis, dehydration is usually the main immediate concern. However, blood in the stool or vomit, green vomit, severe or localised abdominal pain, unusual sleepiness or rapidly worsening illness may signal something more serious. Current guidance has moved firmly away from withholding food, since continuing small amounts of your child''s normal diet alongside fluids supports recovery better than fasting does. Oral rehydration solution — a specific mix of water, salts and sugar — is what actually treats dehydration, more effectively than water alone or juice. Offered in small, frequent sips, it''s the single most useful thing in your toolkit this week; skip juice, soda and other sugary drinks during active illness, since they can worsen diarrhoea rather than help it. The old bland diet of just bananas, rice, applesauce and toast is no longer the recommendation — it''s considered too limited to support real recovery. Once your child is keeping fluids down, their normal, everyday foods in whatever amount they''ll take is the standard advice, and milk and dairy don''t usually need routine avoiding either. Anti-diarrhoeal medicines aren''t recommended for young children without a paediatrician''s direction, and any anti-nausea medicine should come from a clinician rather than a home decision. Call your paediatrician for signs of dehydration or symptoms that drag on, and seek urgent care if your child can''t keep any fluids down at all.',
   'If your child is vomiting or has diarrhoea: offer oral rehydration solution in small, frequent sips rather than large amounts at once, and continue offering your child''s normal foods as they''re able to keep them down. Avoid juice, soda and other sugary drinks until the illness has settled. If your child develops loose stools after drinking milk during the illness: most children don''t need to stop milk or curd during diarrhoea — temporary lactose intolerance can occasionally occur after gastroenteritis. If milk clearly increases bloating or watery stools, speak with your paediatrician before removing dairy or switching products. If you''re considering an anti-diarrhoeal or anti-nausea medicine: use only what your paediatrician has specifically recommended — over-the-counter anti-diarrhoeal medicines carry real risks for young children and aren''t recommended without medical direction. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this is about getting every meal right this week. A brief drop in appetite can happen during a stomach bug — prioritise fluids and offer food without pressure, but seek advice if your child can''t drink, is becoming more unwell, or has persistently poor intake.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Most toddler stomach bugs are viral, self-limited, and pass within about a week — with uncomplicated illness, dehydration is usually the main immediate concern, though a few specific signs are worth watching for too (more on Day 6). Current guidance has moved away from withholding food: continuing small amounts of your child''s normal diet, alongside fluids, actually helps recovery rather than fasting. This week focuses on the fluids that actually treat dehydration, food as your child can tolerate it rather than a restrictive diet, and the signs that mean it''s time to call your paediatrician. Offer small amounts often rather than large meals, and let your child''s appetite guide how much — pushing food when they genuinely don''t want it isn''t necessary.", "breakfast_a": "Mashed banana with a small bowl of curd", "breakfast_b": "Mashed banana with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Plain khichdi with a little ghee", "lunch_b": "Chicken and rice khichdi", "nourishment_break_a": "Plain toast with mashed banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with plain toast", "dinner_a": "Soft rice with a thin moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft rice with finely shredded, fully cooked chicken", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What a stomach bug looks like", "notes": "A typical viral stomach bug brings watery diarrhoea, vomiting and sometimes a mild fever, usually starting to ease within a few days and resolving within about a week. It is unpleasant but ordinarily self-limited. Dehydration is usually the main immediate concern, while blood in the stool or vomit, green vomit, severe abdominal pain, unusual drowsiness or rapidly worsening illness require medical attention. For your child: A child who''s playing, alert and making wet nappies as usual is managing reasonably well, even mid-illness — those are better signs to watch than how much they''ve eaten.", "breakfast_a": "Plain rice porridge with a little ghee", "breakfast_b": "Plain rice porridge with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a grated carrot", "lunch_b": "Chicken khichdi with a grated carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a ripe banana", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a thin dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with finely shredded, fully cooked chicken", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fluids that actually help", "notes": "Oral rehydration solution — a specific mix of water, salts and sugar — is what actually treats dehydration from vomiting and diarrhoea, far better than water alone or juice. If your child is vomiting, offer it in small sips — a teaspoon at a time — rather than a full glass, and increase the amount gradually as it''s tolerated. Use a commercially prepared ORS sachet or ready-to-drink solution whenever available. Mix a sachet with exactly the amount of safe water stated on the packet — never make it stronger or weaker, and never add extra sugar or salt. Use a recognised emergency homemade recipe only when commercial ORS is unavailable and reliable instructions are available. For your child: Juice, soda and other sugary drinks can make diarrhoea worse rather than better, since their sugar content pulls fluid into the gut. Skip these during active illness, even though they may be what your child is asking for.", "breakfast_a": "Suji porridge with mashed banana and no added sugar", "breakfast_b": "Suji porridge with a boiled egg and no added sugar", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked carrot", "lunch_b": "Soft rice with finely shredded, fully cooked chicken and soft-cooked carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a little ghee and soft-cooked spinach", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken and soft-cooked spinach", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Food, as tolerated", "notes": "A bland, restricted diet of just bananas, rice, applesauce and toast is no longer the recommendation — it''s now considered too limited to support real recovery. Once your child is keeping fluids down, offering their normal, everyday foods in whatever amount they''ll take is the standard advice, rather than holding back to a narrow list for days. Milk and dairy don''t usually need to be avoided either; if a particular food seems to upset your child''s stomach further, ease off that one food rather than restricting everything. For your child: Most children don''t need to stop milk or curd during diarrhoea. Temporary lactose intolerance can occasionally occur after gastroenteritis. If milk clearly increases bloating or watery stools, speak with your paediatrician before removing dairy or switching products.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and mashed potato", "lunch_b": "Mild chicken curry with rice and mashed potato", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk", "dinner_a": "Roti with a thin dal and mashed carrot", "dinner_b": "Roti with a light chicken stew and mashed carrot", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Medication safety", "notes": "Do not give an over-the-counter anti-diarrhoeal medicine to a young child unless their paediatrician specifically recommends it. These medicines may cause serious adverse effects and may be unsuitable for the child''s age or the cause of the illness. Anti-nausea medication is sometimes used, but as something a clinician decides on after seeing your child, not a home remedy to start on your own. Worth knowing: Probiotic effects differ by strain and product, and they aren''t routinely needed for a stomach bug. Ask your child''s paediatrician before using one — especially if your child was born prematurely, has reduced immunity or a serious medical condition. Probiotics don''t replace ORS or medical care.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Rice with a thin masoor dal", "lunch_b": "Soft rice with mashed, boneless, fully cooked fish", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of mashed papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of rice porridge with a little ghee", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a little ghee and mashed pumpkin", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken and mashed pumpkin", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to seek care", "notes": "Call your paediatrician for signs of dehydration — noticeably fewer wet nappies, a dry mouth, fewer tears when crying, or your child seeming unusually tired or less playful than normal — or if diarrhoea, fever or vomiting is dragging on without easing. How urgently to act depends on your child''s age, hydration, how severe things look and what other symptoms are present, so when in doubt, it''s worth calling rather than waiting to see. For your child: Get urgent medical care if your child can''t keep any fluids down at all, has blood in vomit or stool, green vomit, looks unusually drowsy or hard to wake, or shows signs of more serious dehydration such as sunken eyes, cool or discoloured hands and feet, or very infrequent urination.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable rava idli", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable rava idli with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Rice with a thin chana dal", "lunch_b": "Mild fish curry with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana with a small bowl of curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Plain, low-salt, developmentally safe crackers with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a thin moong dal and mashed pumpkin", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a light chicken stew and mashed pumpkin", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did keeping fluids going feel more manageable once you weren''t also trying to get full meals in — and is there anything about this week worth remembering for next time?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to trust that a few rough, under-eaten days are not a setback — just a passing stretch your child''s body is working through?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
