-- postpartum week 76: Eating well when your toddler has a cold
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   76,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler has a cold',
   '"A blocked nose can make eating surprisingly difficult — clear the way, keep fluids coming, and let appetite return on its own timeline."',
   'Most toddler colds are viral, ordinary, and ease within about 7 to 10 days without needing special food or medicine. What actually changes around eating during a cold has three separate causes: a blocked nose making it hard to breathe and eat at once, a fever dulling appetite generally, and a sore throat making swallowing uncomfortable. Recognising which one (or ones) are at play helps you pick the right comfort measure rather than just hoping your child eats more. Fluids matter more than food volume this week. Offering plenty to drink keeps your child hydrated, helps thin mucus, and soothes a sore throat when offered warm. Clearing the nose with saline and gentle suction shortly before meals can make a real difference to how well your child manages to eat, since congestion — not lack of hunger — is often what''s getting in the way. Milk and dairy don''t need to be limited during a cold. The idea that dairy increases mucus is a common one, but it isn''t well supported — the coating feeling milk leaves in the mouth is often mistaken for more mucus. Honey can help settle a cough for children 12 months and older, but should never be given under 12 months because of the risk of infant botulism. Over-the-counter cough and cold products, particularly multi-ingredient products containing decongestants, cough suppressants or antihistamines, should not be given to children under 4 unless their clinician specifically recommends a product and dose, and should not be used at all under 2. Most colds don''t need medicine; fluids, comfort and time do the work. Call your paediatrician for high or persistent fever, breathing difficulty, or symptoms that drag on well past the usual week or two — more on the specific signs later this week.',
   'If your child won''t eat much for a day or two: offer small, frequent portions of soft, easy foods and don''t push volume — fluids and comfort matter more than a full plate during a short illness. A brief appetite dip is ordinary and tends to resolve once the cold does. If congestion is making it hard for your child to eat or drink: clear the nose with saline drops and gentle suction or a tissue shortly before meals and at bedtime. A cool-mist humidifier in the sleep space can also help keep mucus thinner overnight. If your child has asthma or a history of wheezing: colds can trigger wheezing or breathing changes more readily than in other children — follow any asthma action plan you''ve been given, and call your paediatrician sooner rather than later if breathing looks different from usual. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this is about getting every meal right this week. A brief drop in appetite during a cold is ordinary — prioritise fluids and comfort, and offer food without pressure while it passes.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Most toddler colds are ordinary, run their course in about 7 to 10 days, and don''t need special food. Eating may become harder because a blocked nose interferes with breathing while chewing, fever reduces appetite, or a sore throat makes swallowing uncomfortable. Different comfort measures match each of these, which is what this week walks through. This week focuses on making eating physically easier, offering fluids and soft comfort foods without pressure, and knowing which signs mean it''s worth a call to your paediatrician rather than just riding it out. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability regardless of appetite — mince or shred meat finely, mash lentils and vegetables softer than usual if congestion makes chewing tiring, cook eggs fully, and supervise all eating closely, since a stuffy nose can also affect how carefully a tired toddler chews.", "breakfast_a": "Warm oats porridge with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Warm oats porridge with a fully cooked scrambled egg", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a little ghee", "lunch_b": "Chicken and rice khichdi, finely shredded", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe pear", "nourishment_break_b": "Warm milk with a small piece of soft cheese", "dinner_a": "Soft rice with a mild moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft rice with a spoon of ghee and finely shredded, fully cooked chicken", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What a toddler cold looks like", "notes": "A typical cold brings a runny or blocked nose, sneezing, a mild fever around 101-102°F (38.3-38.9°C) that''s often worse at night, a scratchy throat, a cough, some fussiness and less interest in food. Nasal discharge commonly turns thicker and yellow or green as a cold runs its course — that colour change on its own is normal and doesn''t mean a bacterial infection or a need for antibiotics. For your child: Toddlers catch a lot of colds — 8 to 10 in the first two years isn''t unusual, since they haven''t yet met most of the viruses that cause them. Frequent colds at this age are ordinary, not a sign anything is wrong with your child''s diet or immunity.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia, soft and warm", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia with a fully cooked boiled egg mashed in", "lunch_a": "Rice with a soft-cooked masoor dal", "lunch_b": "Mild chicken stew with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana, mashed if congestion makes chewing tiring", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft-cooked apple", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a light moong dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a light chicken curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Clearing the way to eat", "notes": "A blocked nose makes it hard to breathe and eat at the same time, since young children rely heavily on nose-breathing while chewing or sucking. Clearing the nose shortly before a meal — saline drops followed by gentle suction or a tissue, depending on what your child tolerates — genuinely makes feeding easier, not just more comfortable. A cool-mist humidifier may help if the air is dry. Place it safely out of reach, empty and refill it with fresh water daily, and clean it exactly as the manufacturer directs to prevent mould and bacteria from building up. For your child: Offering fluids often through the day helps thin mucus as well as prevent dehydration — plain water, milk, or a warm soup are all reasonable choices between meals.", "breakfast_a": "Idli, soft and warm, with a mild sambar", "breakfast_b": "Idli with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Vegetable khichdi with soft-cooked carrot and a little ghee", "lunch_b": "Chicken khichdi with soft-cooked carrot", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of homemade, low-salt vegetable soup, lightly blended", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of homemade, low-salt chicken and rice soup, finely shredded", "dinner_a": "Soft rice with a moong dal and mashed carrot", "dinner_b": "Soft rice with finely flaked, fully cooked fish and mashed carrot", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Soothing a sore throat", "notes": "Soft, moist foods are often easier to manage when a throat is sore. Offer them warm, cool or at room temperature according to what your child finds comfortable. Soup, soft khichdi, curd and well-cooked porridge may be easier than dry crackers or coarse, crunchy foods. Milk and other dairy don''t need to be limited during a cold: despite the common idea that dairy ''increases mucus,'' research hasn''t found that drinking milk actually increases mucus production — the coating feeling in the mouth is often mistaken for it. Dairy can be a genuinely useful source of calories and comfort when appetite is down. Worth knowing: For a child aged 12 months or older with a bothersome cough, ½ teaspoon of honey may be offered plain or in a little warm — not hot — water or milk. It is optional rather than a routine part of the meal plan, and remains a form of added sugar. Never give honey to a child under 12 months, because of the risk of infant botulism. Brush teeth afterward.", "breakfast_a": "Warm suji porridge with stewed apple, no added sugar", "breakfast_b": "Warm suji porridge with a boiled egg on the side, no added sugar", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a little ghee and soft-cooked, lightly mashed green peas", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken and soft-cooked, lightly mashed green peas", "nourishment_break_a": "Warm milk", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe mango", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a thin dal and mashed pumpkin", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a light chicken curry and mashed pumpkin", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Medication safety", "notes": "Over-the-counter cough and cold products, particularly multi-ingredient products containing decongestants, cough suppressants or antihistamines, should not be given to children under 4 unless their clinician specifically recommends a product and dose. Do not use them in children under 2. Beyond the safety concerns, studies haven''t shown these products actually work well in young children. If your child needs relief, ask your paediatrician what''s appropriate, and be careful about combining products, since ingredients like fever reducers can overlap between them. Worth knowing: Do not start high-dose zinc, vitamin C or another supplement specifically for a toddler''s cold unless their paediatrician recommends it. Supplements can have side effects, interact with medicines and do not replace ordinary food, fluids or medical care. Continue offering normal fruit and vegetables as tolerated.", "breakfast_a": "Warm semolina upma with soft-cooked vegetables", "breakfast_b": "Warm semolina upma with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a thin chana dal and mashed sweet potato", "lunch_b": "Mild fish curry with rice and mashed sweet potato", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of homemade, low-salt tomato and lentil soup, lightly blended", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of soft-cooked oats", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a little ghee and mashed carrot", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken and mashed carrot", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to seek care", "notes": "Call your paediatrician if fever climbs above 104°F (40°C), if fever lasts more than a day in a child under 2 (or more than three days at any age), if a runny or blocked nose hasn''t improved after 10 to 14 days, if a cough drags on well past that, or if your child seems to be improving and then gets worse again. Ear-tugging with fussiness, drainage from the ear, or pain on swallowing that seems out of proportion to a typical sore throat are also worth a call, since colds can lead into an ear infection. For your child: Get urgent medical care for fast or laboured breathing, ribs or chest pulling in with each breath, a whistling or high-pitched sound when breathing, a harsh sound when breathing in that continues even when your child is calm, bluish lips or fingernails, or if your child can''t keep fluids down or shows signs of dehydration — a dry mouth, no tears when crying, or noticeably fewer wet nappies.", "breakfast_a": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared per the label, with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared per the label, alongside a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Soft rice with a masoor dal and mashed potato", "lunch_b": "Roti with a light chicken curry and mashed potato", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with mashed ripe papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "Plain, low-salt crackers, sized for safe chewing, with a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with well-cooked, lightly mashed rajma curry", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a light egg curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did clearing the nose before meals make a difference — and is there a comfort food or warm drink worth remembering for the next cold?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to trust that a few quiet, low-appetite days during a cold are the illness passing through — not something you need to fix by getting your child to eat more?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 77: Eating well as your toddler learns to feed themselves
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   77,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well as your toddler learns to feed themselves',
   '"The mess is proof of practice — the skill is coming, even on the messiest days."',
   'Somewhere in the second year, most toddlers move from needing to be fed to genuinely wanting to feed themselves — with fingers first, a spoon soon after, and a fork usually later and less consistently. Spilling, dropping, and flinging food are normal parts of this stage, not signs of a problem, and real messiness commonly continues well into the third year for many children. A simple way to think about your role: you decide what food is offered, when, and where; your child decides whether to eat it and how much. Letting your child own the whether-and-how-much side — including the mess that comes with self-feeding it — tends to make mealtimes calmer and gives your child real room to practise the skill, rather than turning eating into something you''re both fighting to control. A few practical choices make this easier: unbreakable, suction-based dishware, a short plastic spoon, and a splash mat under the high chair. Prepare food in a shape, size and texture appropriate to your child''s current chewing and swallowing skills — soft strips may be easier to grasp for some foods, while small, soft pieces may suit a child with an established pincer grasp; avoid hard, round, firm, sticky or difficult-to-chew pieces. Safe preparation and active, close supervision work together: keep your child seated upright and watch continuously while they eat. Most of what you''ll see — a child who''s faster, slower, neater or messier than another — is ordinary variation. It''s worth a word with your paediatrician if your child can''t bring food to their mouth at all well past the usual age range, shows no gradual improvement with practice, or gags, coughs or loses food far more than seems typical — more on the specific signs later this week.',
   'If your child still relies mostly on your help to eat: that''s within the normal range at this age — offer chances to practise at every meal without pressure, and let progress happen at your child''s own pace rather than comparing it to another child''s. If mealtime mess is a source of stress for you: unbreakable dishware, a splash mat, and deciding in advance to clean up at the end of the meal rather than mid-meal can make self-feeding meals genuinely lower-effort, even though the mess itself won''t disappear yet. If gagging during self-feeding worries you: gagging commonly involves noise, coughing, retching or a red face and often happens while learning a new texture; choking may involve an ineffective or absent cough, inability to breathe, cry or make sound, or a change in colour. If you suspect choking, act immediately with age-appropriate first aid — never a blind finger sweep — and make sure all caregivers have learned infant and child choking first aid and CPR from a recognised provider. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this is about getting every meal neat or tidy this week. A messy, slow, independent bite is doing real developmental work, even when it doesn''t look like much progress in the moment.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas. Serve the mother an appropriate adult portion, and adapt the toddler''s portion separately for their chewing, swallowing and self-feeding abilities. "If you''re still hungry" refers to the mother; offer the child additional food according to their own appetite.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Somewhere around now, most toddlers are getting a real feel for the spoon, using their fingers with real skill, and drinking from an open cup with some spilling — fork use tends to arrive a little later and stay inconsistent for a while. None of this is fast or tidy at first, and it isn''t supposed to be: fumbling, dropping, and flinging food are all normal exploring, not misbehaviour. This week is about handing over more of the actual eating to your child — what that looks like day to day, how to set things up so the mess is manageable, and which food-safety habits matter more once your child controls their own bite size. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability regardless of how independently they''re eating it — mince or shred meat finely, cook eggs fully, cut grapes and cherry or grape tomatoes lengthwise into quarters, remove cherry stones completely and cut the fruit lengthwise into quarters or smaller developmentally appropriate pieces, cut sausages or hot dogs lengthwise into quarters and then into small pieces rather than coin-shaped rounds, and supervise all eating closely, since self-feeding shifts more control over bite size to your child.", "breakfast_a": "Soft dosa strips with a smooth coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Soft dosa strips with a boiled egg, cut into strips", "lunch_a": "Thick rice-and-moong khichdi offered on a preloaded spoon", "lunch_b": "Thick chicken-and-rice khichdi offered on a preloaded spoon", "nourishment_break_a": "Ripe mango, cut into finger-sized strips", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely diced ripe mango", "dinner_a": "Soft roti strips with a mashed rajma curry for dipping", "dinner_b": "Soft roti strips with a mashed egg curry for dipping", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What''s actually happening", "notes": "You''re responsible for what food is offered, when, and where; your child is responsible for whether they eat it and how much. Handing over the how-much part is exactly what makes room for self-feeding to develop — a toddler who''s deciding their own pace and quantity is also the one practising the motor skills that make eating more independent over time. For your child: Pressuring a child to eat a particular food tends to make them like it less, not more — offering it without comment, alongside foods you know they''ll eat, works better over time than coaxing or insisting.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli pieces with a smooth tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli pieces with finely shredded omelette mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft paneer, finely crumbled into steamed rice", "lunch_b": "Soft, shredded, boneless fish, carefully checked for fine bones, with steamed rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed sweet potato, cut into finger-sized strips", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed steamed sweet potato", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-lentil cutlets, soft-cooked, cut into strips", "dinner_b": "Chicken and vegetable cutlets, soft-cooked, cut into strips", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Setting up for success", "notes": "A few practical choices make self-feeding easier for both of you: an unbreakable, suction-based bowl that resists tipping, a short plastic spoon your child can grip easily, and a splash mat or old sheet under the high chair so cleanup is quick rather than a production. Loading the spoon yourself and letting your child carry it the rest of the way to their mouth is a genuinely useful bridge while full spoon-loading is still developing. For your child: Eating together lets your child watch you use a spoon, fork, and cup — that everyday modelling does real work, more than any amount of instruction or correction in the moment.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam strips with a smooth coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam strips with a boiled egg, cut into strips", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked cauliflower and potato sabzi with rice", "lunch_b": "Minced chicken and potato sabzi with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A very ripe, soft pear, cut into small pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with finely diced, very ripe pear", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha strips with a mild dal for dipping", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha strips with a mild chicken curry for dipping", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Foods that build the skill", "notes": "Food prepared in a shape, size and texture appropriate to your child''s current chewing and swallowing skills is genuinely easier to pick up and manage — soft strips may suit some foods, while small, soft pieces may suit a child with an established pincer grasp; avoid hard, round, firm, sticky or difficult-to-chew pieces regardless of size. A plate with a few different shapes and textures gives your child real chances to practise picking up, loading a spoon, and managing a bite, even if a good portion of it ends up on the floor. It''s fine to offer a mix of foods your child can manage independently alongside one you still help with — self-feeding doesn''t have to be all-or-nothing at any single meal.", "breakfast_a": "Soft French toast, cooked until the egg mixture is fully set, cut into strips, with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Soft French toast, cooked until the egg mixture is fully set, cut into strips, with a boiled egg, cut into strips", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked carrot and potato mash with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked carrot and potato mash with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed zucchini, cut into finger-sized strips", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed steamed zucchini", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi, soft and warm", "dinner_b": "Chicken khichdi, soft and warm", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Choking safety while self-feeding", "notes": "Because a self-feeding toddler controls their own bite size, safe preparation and active, close supervision both matter — neither one is enough on its own. Cut grapes and cherry or grape tomatoes lengthwise into quarters, and remove cherry stones completely and cut the fruit lengthwise into quarters or smaller developmentally appropriate pieces. Cut sausages or hot dogs lengthwise into quarters and then into small pieces — never into coin-shaped rounds. Cook hard vegetables until soft enough to pierce easily with a fork, and spread nut or seed butter thinly rather than offering it by the spoonful. Size alone doesn''t make a food safe: hard, round, firm, sticky or difficult-to-chew pieces need extra care whatever their size. Worth knowing: Gagging commonly involves noise, coughing, retching or a red face, and often happens while a child is learning a new texture. Choking may involve an ineffective or absent cough, inability to breathe, cry or make sound, or a change in colour. If you suspect choking, act immediately using age-appropriate first aid — never a blind finger sweep — and make sure all caregivers have learned infant and child choking first aid and CPR from a recognised provider. Always supervise self-feeding meals, keep your child seated rather than walking or lying down while eating, and skip food-related games or distractions during meals.", "breakfast_a": "Soft pancake strips with mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Soft pancake strips with a boiled egg, cut into strips", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked pumpkin cubes with rice and a little ghee", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked pumpkin cubes with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Cucumber, peeled and finely grated", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with peeled, finely grated cucumber", "dinner_a": "Soft roti strips with a mild moong dal for dipping", "dinner_b": "Soft roti strips with a mild, boneless fish curry (carefully checked for fine bones) for dipping", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to check in with your paediatrician", "notes": "Most of the range you''ll see — a toddler who''s faster or slower, neater or messier, more or less interested in the spoon than another child — is ordinary variation and not something to worry about. It''s worth mentioning to your paediatrician if your child still can''t bring food to their mouth at all well past the usual age range, shows no gradual improvement with practice over weeks and months, or gags, coughs or loses food from their mouth far more than seems typical. For your child: Raise concerns with your paediatrician if meals regularly last longer than about 30 minutes; your child repeatedly coughs, chokes or develops a wet or congested voice during meals; food remains pocketed in the mouth; food or liquid is frequently lost from the mouth; only one or two textures are tolerated; eating causes marked distress; or growth, hydration or energy is affected. A preference for familiar foods, ordinary messiness or a slower learning pace alone does not mean your child has a feeding disorder.", "breakfast_a": "Soft banana pancake strips", "breakfast_b": "Soft banana pancake strips with a boiled egg on the side, cut into strips", "lunch_a": "Rice and vegetable pulao, soft-cooked", "lunch_b": "Rice and chicken pulao, soft-cooked", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed carrot, cut into finger-sized strips", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with mashed steamed carrot", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a little ghee and well-cooked, lightly mashed beans", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken and well-cooked, lightly mashed beans", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did handing over more of the actual eating feel easier than expected, or harder — and is there one small setup change worth keeping for next week?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to see the mess on the floor as evidence your child is genuinely learning, rather than something you did wrong?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 78: Eating well when your toddler is gaining weight slowly
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   78,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your toddler is gaining weight slowly',
   '"A slower climb on the growth curve isn''t solved by pushing harder — steady, calorie-rich food offered without a fight does more than pressure ever will."',
   'Growth slows dramatically in the second year — most toddlers gain only around three to five pounds across the entire year, a huge deceleration from tripling their birth weight in year one. Appetite also becomes noticeably more variable: a child may eat considerably more on one day and less on another without anything necessarily being wrong. What matters on a growth chart is the trend over time, not a single measurement: a child who''s always tracked at a lower percentile is usually fine there, while a child moving downward away from a curve they used to follow is the more meaningful signal worth a paediatrician''s attention. This week focuses on a few deliberate, nutrient-dense additions — a small amount of ghee or oil, full-fat dairy, nut or seed butter, avocado or egg — added once or twice a day to food your child already eats, alongside a rhythm of food roughly every two to three hours and not filling up on milk or juice before meals. None of this is about pressuring your child to eat more: you decide what''s offered, when, and where; your child still decides whether and how much, and pressure tends to backfire rather than help. A toddler eating less overall is at somewhat higher risk of falling short on iron, vitamin D and zinc, which is part of why nutrient-dense additions matter more than just extra calories of any kind. A routine multivitamin isn''t automatically needed just because a child is smaller, but vitamin D is worth asking about specifically, since food alone doesn''t always provide the 600 IU a day children this age need — your paediatrician can advise based on your child''s diet and milk intake. Contact your paediatrician if your child is moving downward away from their previous growth pattern, has very low weight relative to length or height, is losing weight, or has slow gain together with persistent vomiting, diarrhoea, feeding difficulty or other symptoms. Interpretation depends on accurate serial measurements, age, prematurity, family growth pattern and the chart being used — many children ultimately need only practical feeding support, but assessment is what distinguishes ordinary variation from a genuine concern. More on the specific signs later this week. Using this shared menu These are family meal ideas: serve the mother an appropriate adult portion and enrich the toddler''s portion separately, only when needed — "if you''re still hungry" refers to the mother, and the toddler''s own extras shouldn''t be added automatically to her meal too, since they may not suit her individual diabetes, blood-pressure or cholesterol guidance. Where the menu adds ghee, oil, or another calorie-dense extra for the toddler, "a small amount" means an amount suited to their own portion, not an adult-sized spoonful — ask your paediatrician what amount and frequency fits your child if growth is a genuine concern.',
   'If you''re comparing your child''s size to another child''s: growth reflects genetics, family size and your child''s own pattern, not just food — a smaller-framed child of smaller-framed parents tracking lower on the curve is a different situation from a child falling away from their own established line. Comparing to a cousin or classmate isn''t a meaningful gauge either way. If mealtimes have started to feel like a battle over how much your child eats: stepping back from persuading, bribing or pressuring — even though it feels counterintuitive when you''re worried about weight — tends to help appetite recover rather than hurt it. You''re still responsible for offering nourishing, calorie-dense food; your child is still responsible for how much of it they eat. If your child was born preterm or has an underlying health condition: growth expectations and the right chart to use may differ for your child — ask your paediatrician how your child''s particular growth pattern should be interpreted rather than comparing it to standard milestones. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. None of this is about getting your child to eat more through effort or willpower this week. Offering calorie-rich food without pressure, consistently, is doing real work even on days it doesn''t look like much changed.',
   'Meat, fish and egg are optional in every meal this week — swap in the vegetarian option freely. When you do include them, cook them all the way through.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Growth naturally slows down a lot in the second year — most toddlers gain only around three to five pounds across the whole year, compared with tripling their birth weight in year one. Appetite also becomes noticeably more variable: your child may eat considerably more on one day and less on another without anything necessarily being wrong. This week focuses on a few deliberate, nutrient-dense additions to food your child already likes, building a rhythm that supports appetite, and knowing what''s worth flagging to your paediatrician versus what''s ordinary variation. Prepare every food for your child''s current chewing ability — mince or shred meat finely, cook eggs fully, cut round foods like grapes or cherry tomatoes lengthwise into quarters, and supervise all eating closely.", "breakfast_a": "Warm oats porridge with a small amount of ghee and mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Warm oats porridge with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked vegetables", "lunch_b": "Rice with minced chicken and soft-cooked vegetables", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of full-fat yogurt with a small amount of smooth peanut butter mixed in", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of full-fat yogurt with mashed banana", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma curry", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with tender, finely minced or finely shredded, fully cooked mutton curry", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of full-fat yogurt"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Understanding the growth chart", "notes": "What matters on a growth chart isn''t a single number, it''s the trend over time — a child who''s always tracked at a lower percentile is usually just fine there, while a child who''s fallen away from a curve they used to follow is the pattern that''s worth a closer look. Percentile isn''t a grade to maximise; it''s more like consistency being the goal, not a particular score. For your child: It''s completely reasonable to ask your paediatrician to walk you through your child''s growth chart at your next visit — seeing the actual trend, rather than guessing at a single number, is usually more reassuring than worrying alone.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia with grated cheese stirred in", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a mashed sweet potato", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken and a mashed sweet potato", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed avocado on soft toast fingers", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed avocado on soft toast fingers with a boiled egg on the side", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a paneer bhurji", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an egg bhurji", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Calorie-dense additions that count", "notes": "A deliberate, nutrient-dense addition to a dish your child already eats — a small amount of ghee or oil, full-fat dairy, nut or seed butter, avocado, or an egg — adds real calories without needing to change the whole meal. One or two such additions a day are usually enough; the rest of the day''s food can stay as it normally would. Small, easy swaps work well too: full-fat rather than reduced-fat dairy, cheese melted over vegetables, and well-cooked, tender protein your child can manage easily. For your child: None of this needs to be a separate project — the goal is simply richer versions of foods your child is already willing to eat, not a whole new menu, and not every meal needs an added extra.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with finely ground peanuts stirred through, if peanut has already been introduced safely", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with finely ground peanuts stirred through and a fully cooked egg", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked pumpkin and rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked pumpkin and rice with shredded chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of full-fat curd with mashed ripe banana", "nourishment_break_b": "Cheese melted over soft-steamed cauliflower", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a lower-mercury fish curry, boneless and carefully checked for fine bones", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of full-fat curd with mashed banana"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A rhythm that helps", "notes": "Offer three meals and two or three planned snacks at reasonably predictable times, often about two to three hours apart, with water between them — this supports appetite without creating continuous grazing. Milk can fill a toddler up quickly: for many children past their first birthday, roughly 16 to 24 ounces (about 475-710 mL) a day is the usual upper range, and excessive milk may displace iron-rich food — ask your paediatrician what amount suits your child. If 100% fruit juice is offered at all, keep it to no more than 4 ounces (120 mL) a day, served in an open cup with a meal, and prefer whole fruit when you can. Worth knowing: None of this is about getting your child to eat more through pressure or persuasion — you''re still in charge of what''s offered, when, and where; your child stays in charge of whether and how much. Pushing tends to backfire and can make mealtimes harder, not easier, over time.", "breakfast_a": "Soft suji upma with grated carrot", "breakfast_b": "Soft suji upma with shredded omelette", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked spinach and potato sabzi with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked spinach and potato sabzi with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of full-fat curd with a small amount of smooth peanut butter mixed in", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of full-fat curd with grated cheese mixed in", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi with a small amount of ghee", "dinner_b": "Chicken khichdi with a small amount of ghee", "still_hungry": "Hummus with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Nutrients worth extra attention", "notes": "A toddler eating less overall is at somewhat higher risk of falling short on iron, vitamin D and zinc, even if total calories aren''t dramatically low — which is part of why nutrient-dense additions matter more than simply piling on empty calories. Iron-fortified cereal, lean meat, beans and lentils; vitamin D from fortified milk and fatty fish; and a variety of protein sources for zinc are all reasonable food-first ways to cover these. Worth knowing: A routine multivitamin isn''t automatically needed just because a child is smaller. Vitamin D is a separate consideration: children aged 12 to 24 months need 600 IU a day, and food alone doesn''t always provide enough. Ask your paediatrician whether your child''s diet, fortified milk intake and individual circumstances point to a vitamin D or other supplement.", "breakfast_a": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared with whole milk and mashed banana", "breakfast_b": "Plain, iron-fortified toddler cereal with no added sugar, prepared with whole milk and a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a lentil dal and a soft-cooked tomato-bell pepper sabzi", "lunch_b": "Rice with minced mutton and a soft-cooked tomato-bell pepper sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Orange segments, seeds and tough membrane removed, cut into soft pieces, with a small bowl of full-fat curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Orange segments, seeds and tough membrane removed, cut into soft pieces, with a small bowl of full-fat curd and a small amount of ground flaxseed stirred in", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma curry and a soft-cooked spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an egg curry and a soft-cooked spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of full-fat yogurt with a small amount of ground flaxseed"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to loop in your paediatrician", "notes": "Contact your paediatrician if your child is moving downward away from their previous growth pattern, has very low weight relative to length or height, is losing weight, or has slow gain together with persistent vomiting, diarrhoea, feeding difficulty or other symptoms. Interpretation depends on accurate serial measurements, your child''s age, prematurity, family growth pattern and the chart being used, so this is genuinely a job for your paediatrician rather than something to judge from a single home weigh-in. Many children ultimately need practical feeding support, but the right next step depends on the specific growth pattern and any accompanying symptoms — assessment is what distinguishes ordinary variation from a feeding, nutritional or medical concern. For your child: Developmental delay alongside poor growth, or a child who seems unusually tired, unwell, or uninterested in their surroundings rather than just a picky or light eater, is also worth raising promptly rather than waiting for the next scheduled visit.", "breakfast_a": "Soft dosa with a smooth peanut chutney, if peanut has already been introduced safely", "breakfast_b": "Soft dosa with a boiled egg on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft-cooked mixed vegetable curry with rice", "lunch_b": "Soft-cooked mixed vegetable curry with shredded chicken and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of full-fat curd with grated cheese", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of full-fat curd with mashed banana", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a small amount of ghee and mashed potato", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with shredded chicken and mashed potato", "still_hungry": "A slice of paneer with soft toast fingers"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Did a particular calorie-dense addition feel easy to keep offering — and is there one small habit worth carrying into next week?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to trust that steady, pressure-free meals are quietly doing the right work for your child''s growth, even without a number on a scale to prove it week to week?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your child''s nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;
